WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.448 --> 00:00:01.281
- I almost think that that's why

00:00:01.281 --> 00:00:03.410
I'm so successful out there.

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.110
It's 'cause I'm only inside.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.550
And for me, there is no winning,
there is no smelling roses.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.320
One of the things I challenge myself

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.720
is to get a little bit
better at smelling roses.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.300
But I'm not even sure that's right,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.110
because I think the process is the rose.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.610
You know, just playing is what I'm up to.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:22.828
I just wanna play.

00:00:22.828 --> 00:00:25.040
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.513
- You got your perspective.

00:00:27.489 --> 00:00:28.322
(crowd cheering)

00:00:29.926 --> 00:00:32.200
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.370
(upbeat pop music)

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.640
- Can you tell us something
really down to earth about you?

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.590
- Well it's funny, even the nature

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.212
of the question makes me uncomfortable.

00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:42.878
(laughing)

00:00:42.878 --> 00:00:44.650
So the meta of the question is probably

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.150
the most down to earth thing about me.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.070
Meaning, I, it's actually
what I think a lot about.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.700
My favorite thing is what people

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.970
who actually have met
me say behind my back.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.010
That's the real answer.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.490
I mean, the most down to
earth thing about me is

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.340
my parents and my circumstance
created a framework

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.780
where I'm unable to hear the cheering.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.060
You know, and so I think one
of the reasons I deal well

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:08.972
with negative feedback is because

00:01:08.972 --> 00:01:12.080
of the way I deal with positive feedback.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.060
I think the reason so many
people struggle with trolls

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:15.903
or negative comments or judgment

00:01:15.903 --> 00:01:20.434
is because they also
overvalue compliments.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:24.530
So, for me, I think it's that I have both

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:26.222
of my index fingers in my ears.

00:01:26.222 --> 00:01:27.170
(laughs)

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.340
What I mean by that is I
just don't hear either.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.150
And so for me humility is the thing

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.440
I'm most attracted to
within myself and in others.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.060
And so, yeah, just being
uncomfortable with the question

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.900
I think is the most down
to earth thing about me.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.920
- Yeah (laughs), I love that so much.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.781
I think in every, of all the content,

00:01:46.781 --> 00:01:48.120
I mean, what I try to do with each guest

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.890
is research everything I can about them.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:50.982
There is way-

00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:51.820
- There's a lot.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.741
- Too much content on the
internet, too, about everything.

00:01:52.741 --> 00:01:53.574
- Yes.

00:01:53.574 --> 00:01:55.150
- But your humility and even calling,

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.053
you know, the latest wine company Empathy.

00:01:56.053 --> 00:01:57.245
- Yes.

00:01:57.245 --> 00:01:59.330
That alone is something
that shines through

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.291
so strongly that you have.

00:02:00.291 --> 00:02:01.124
- Thank you.

00:02:01.124 --> 00:02:01.957
You know, come from what you call typical

00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:02.790
immigrant upbringing and I love

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.320
that it stuck with you the whole time.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.330
- Yeah me too, me too.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.310
I think one of the things
I most struggle with.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.256
You know, I think fame and money

00:02:12.256 --> 00:02:15.930
and awareness exposes people.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.840
I don't think it really changes people.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.980
And I think it really
is sad to me to watch

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.180
contemporaries or friends or acquaintances

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.650
when they do hit a little bit
more awareness or more money

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.300
that there is an evolution
in their personality.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.940
And I think that, I don't
think that's a change,

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.029
I think that's who they
actually were the whole time.

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:34.862
- Yeah.

00:02:34.862 --> 00:02:37.880
- So, yeah, I'm very grateful

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.630
that I don't have that DNA trait

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.540
that makes me think I'm somebody

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.420
just because I may have some financial

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.060
or large following success.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.180
- Yeah, well, what also is in your DNA

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.520
is this entrepreneurial trait

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.840
and this incredible ability
to be ahead of the curve

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.670
and to see, have this
intuition that lets you see

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.060
what other people don't, you know?

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.720
What the next five, 10
years are gonna look like.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:00.553
- Yes.

00:03:00.553 --> 00:03:02.190
- So in terms of your way
to yay, that's pretty much

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.890
the story of you got to a
life that makes you yay.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.490
Take us back to the very beginning

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.490
because I think that
anyone who's looking to you

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.880
for guidance of how to
find what lights you up

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.020
and our lives, we're expected to have

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.880
so many different careers
in our lifetime now.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.400
It's so overwhelming.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.200
Take us back to young Gary.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.107
Like, born in the Soviet Union.

00:03:20.107 --> 00:03:22.110
- Born in the Soviet
Union, moved to Queens

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.400
for a couple years then
moved to New Jersey.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.310
Um, very close to his mom,
Dad's working every minute.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.860
Doesn't really have him in
his life the first 15 years

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.890
'cause he just worked so hard.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.723
14 years.

00:03:32.723 --> 00:03:34.460
And then I started working
at my dad's liquor stores

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.520
so I got to really know him.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.520
But just quickly gravitated
towards business.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.580
You know, six, seven, eight years old.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.101
Lemonade stand, shoveling
snow, washing people cars.

00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:46.934
- Baseball cards.

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:48.850
- Baseball cards, you
know all those things

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:53.030
and so much like somebody
is a natural athlete

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.980
or a natural singer-songwriter.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:58.490
You know, every time I
watch videos of young Lebron

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:02.560
or young Beyonce, I always
think man, I wish my mom,

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.000
I wish there was recordings
of me at baseball card shows

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.880
or behind the lemonade
stand at eight years old.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.640
There was a natural talent
that I had in selling

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.790
and so, yes, I, you
know it's so interesting

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.233
to be in the prime of my life

00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:19.210
during the rise of entrepreneurship.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.650
- You know, it's on a
pedestal, it's cool now.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.140
You know, it's crazy for me because

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.054
when I was selling stuff in high school,

00:04:26.054 --> 00:04:28.367
it was dorky, it was nerdy, it was like-

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:29.200
- Yeah, was it weird?

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.130
- Making money was not
cool, it was being good

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.460
at school or being good at sports.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.230
So, it's very weird,
it's very weird for me

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:40.270
to have admiration or
recognize that rappers

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:43.810
and athletes and actors
and actresses all wanna

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.819
be entrepreneurs now and so it's very cool

00:04:47.819 --> 00:04:50.390
in a lot of ways but I also have concerns

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.580
about people forcing themselves
into entrepreneurship

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.800
when I know that it's really
lonely, it's really difficult

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.210
and it's just not for everybody.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.660
- Yeah, well I love, you
know, I was listening to

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.230
I think it was two years
ago you were on Joe Rogan

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.010
and one of the things you
pinpointed about yourself

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.900
that makes your DNA,
entrepreneurial to your DNA

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.350
not just in aspiration
is that six year old Gary

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.250
had Gary's Lemonade
Stand, had multiple venues

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.670
and you weren't just a kid
who wanted to sell stuff.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.280
You were looking more at
where you would put the sign

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.410
to get the most sales funneled
towards the lemonade stand.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:25.629
I don't even know how a six year old

00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:27.100
could conceive of those ideas.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.870
- It took me 30 years to even know that.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.900
I was giving a speech and I just kinda

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.350
was giving my same
standard first five minutes

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:36.650
of my speech and I just
remember it very clear.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:39.130
I just stopped, I'm like, wait a minute,

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.680
I used to sit, I mean how, I
mean this is just like, weird.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.191
I used to sit and watch
these cars drive by

00:05:46.191 --> 00:05:49.550
and literally take my sign and move it.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.690
Drock's in the background
right now filming some stuff

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.610
and he got to see me in
action this last week

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.250
in Chicago at a baseball card show.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.750
It's the first time I
was a dealer in 25 years

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:03.260
and I mean, the way I was
moving around the tables

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.690
and changing stuff and changing
prices and signs and angles.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.280
You know, that's what I did and that's

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.380
what I do in content
and on these platforms.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:14.060
The way I think about
LinkedIn and Facebook

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.710
and Twitter and YouTube and my podcast

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.735
and email service and
text messaging platform.

00:06:17.735 --> 00:06:19.590
It's a lot of the same thing.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.910
It's attention hacking,
it's attention arbitrage.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:25.610
And it's not only attention.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.190
Hey, here's the best part of my table

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.820
or hey, here's Instagram.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.800
It's also what am I gonna say.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.930
What's the language in the sign,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:37.163
what's the copy I'm gonna put,
what's the title of the meme.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.430
It is something I've always done.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.350
- Yeah, it's cool watching your story,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.908
how it literally just
from strength to strength

00:06:44.908 --> 00:06:48.270
evolved through from six, from
your first business at six

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.370
to everything that you've since touched.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.622
So, you moved into the
family business at 14.

00:06:52.622 --> 00:06:53.455
- Yes, yes.

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:54.288
- Discount Liquors.

00:06:54.288 --> 00:06:55.121
- Yes.

00:06:55.121 --> 00:06:55.954
- Did a little rebrand.

00:06:55.954 --> 00:06:56.787
- Yes.

00:06:56.787 --> 00:06:57.620
- And it became the Wine Library.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:58.453
- Yes.

00:06:58.453 --> 00:07:01.240
- So a deep love for wine and sales and--

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:02.073
- Collecting.

00:07:02.073 --> 00:07:02.960
- And collecting.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.630
- Very big theme of my
life, collecting wine

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.890
was a huge reason I fell in love with it

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.667
because I was transitioning
off collecting cards.

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:11.890
- Baseball cards, yeah.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.410
- It was a big, big,
big, theme of my life.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.710
- And I mean, you turned that business

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.680
into e-commerce before
e-commerce was like a word.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.240
How did you do that.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.600
I mean, how do you
firstly upskill without.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.670
I mean, you went to college,
you went through school

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.970
but how do you upskill to knowing
before there's a Gary Vee.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:32.251
I mean, who was your Gary Vee, firtsly.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.572
At your age was there a you who was-

00:07:34.572 --> 00:07:35.405
- No.

00:07:35.405 --> 00:07:36.238
- Prolific in content.
- No.

00:07:36.238 --> 00:07:37.406
- And democratizing.

00:07:37.406 --> 00:07:39.060
- Let me rephrase, I'm sure.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.520
I mean, actually somebody who I'm very

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.270
fond of historically is Barbara Walters.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.590
I thought Barbara Walters
was an incredible journalist

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:50.330
and incredibly smart
about culture and people.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:53.618
But I didn't look up to business people.

00:07:53.618 --> 00:07:56.800
Leveling up did not happen in school.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.310
It came in the trenches, in the dirt.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:04.310
And the internet, I had
probably been on a computer,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.630
on a computer for less
than two hours in my life

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.418
when I decided to launch winelibrary.com.

00:08:11.418 --> 00:08:14.351
- Oh my god, I mean, YouTube
had been like four months.

00:08:14.351 --> 00:08:17.250
- No, YouTube didn't come out
until ten years after that.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.083
- Oh my gosh!

00:08:18.083 --> 00:08:18.990
- Wine Library TV.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:19.940
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.220
- Was four months after YouTube.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:23.530
This is 1996, ten years before YouTube

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:25.173
came out, nine and a half.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.113
I just inherently understand people.

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:31.120
Like, deeply.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.960
Same reason I knew that VR wasn't

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:34.050
gonna be big in a year or two.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.670
Two years ago when everybody
was going on that bandwagon.

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:38.720
I would, I look, I watch.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.890
I'm watching what your fiance's wearing.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.727
Like that, I literally, that's what I do.

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:43.840
[Man] How am I doing?

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:44.673
You look great.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.970
I look at the haircut he's wearing,

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.640
I look at like everything,
I watch, I watch.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.470
I'm an anthropologist.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:56.530
I think if my DNA was
slightly to the left or right

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:59.260
I'd be one of those people
that goes into the Amazon

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.610
and for 29 years of their career

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:04.450
follows red headed ants in the Amazon.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.420
I'm that curious by behavior.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.850
Luckily for me, well not
luckily, for me the way

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:14.181
that's manifested is in
people and then trends

00:09:14.181 --> 00:09:17.160
and behaviors around business.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.769
But that maps to how we
look at social issues,

00:09:19.769 --> 00:09:23.830
politics, which diseases or issue.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.205
Like, why are we passionate right now

00:09:26.205 --> 00:09:29.293
about plastic straws to paper straws.

00:09:29.293 --> 00:09:32.776
I'm fascinated by why
that's the issue we decide

00:09:32.776 --> 00:09:37.776
while textiles continue to
do enormous damage to Earth

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.230
and we've got people who are yelling

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.600
about the environment wearing Gucci belts.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:44.673
I'm fascinated by that.

00:09:44.673 --> 00:09:47.900
What you choose to do,
what you choose not to do.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:52.210
You know, why coffee over
tea in our culture today.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.980
Just those kind, I'm always thinking

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.190
but most of all, and this
is where humility comes in,

00:09:58.190 --> 00:09:59.830
and I'm gonna round this up.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:01.923
I never think I get to pick.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:05.010
I never get to impose.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.170
I don't think I get to
say what it should be

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.240
and I think a lot of
people think that way.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.410
I watch and just react
to what people are doing.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.230
- I love that you just ask the questions

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.420
that kind of give us the answers

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.940
rather than just hammering
answers down our throat.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.360
You invite conversation
and you use your platforms

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:22.470
to really get us to think about.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.070
I'm an anthropology nerd as well.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:27.300
I did Law Arts but I did
all my theses on like

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.213
studying what people
do and why they do it.

00:10:29.213 --> 00:10:31.670
It's why I find like war history and USSR-

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:32.503
- Yes.

00:10:32.503 --> 00:10:33.336
All that stuff is so interesting.

00:10:33.336 --> 00:10:35.440
- It's huge to me right,
like I leave a place

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.710
of communism, I go to
a place of capitalism.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:41.420
You know, I'm also driven by gratitude.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.050
I genuinely think if me
and my grandfather's,

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:46.400
both of them for that
matter, switched spots,

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.260
I'm the one who spends 10
years in a communist jail,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.580
they're the ones who get
accolades in the current world.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:55.318
